---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy and Cookie Notice | CMNA"
  description="Privacy and cookie notice for cmna.jameshoward.us."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Privacy and Cookie Notice</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        This notice explains what information may be processed when you use
        cmna.jameshoward.us, why it is processed, and what choices you have.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell content">
      <p>
        This is a personal and professional website operated by James P. Howard, II.
        It is the web companion to <em>Computational Methods for Numerical Analysis with R</em>.
        Questions about this notice or the handling of information associated with
        this website may be sent to me through a contact method published on
        <a href="https://jameshoward.us/">jameshoward.us</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Information processed automatically</h2>
      <p>
        Like most websites, CMNA and its hosting infrastructure may process limited
        technical information when a page or resource is requested. This may include
        your Internet Protocol address, the date and time of the request, the page or
        resource requested, a referring page supplied by your browser, browser and
        device information, and information needed to operate or protect the site.
      </p>
      <p>
        The website is hosted through GitHub Pages. Ordinary hosting, delivery,
        security, and diagnostic logs may therefore be processed by GitHub under its
        own terms and privacy practices.
      </p>

      <h2>R code and browser laboratories</h2>
      <p>
        The CMNA laboratories use webR to execute R through WebAssembly in your
        browser. The R code you type into a CMNA laboratory or the R Workbench is not
        sent to a CMNA computation server for execution.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some laboratory resources are loaded from third-party infrastructure. webR is
        loaded from the webR project distribution service, and the optional source
        drawer retrieves canonical CMNA package source from GitHub only when you ask
        to view it. Those providers may receive ordinary request information such as
        your IP address and browser information when your browser requests their
        resources.
      </p>
      <p>
        Shareable laboratory states are encoded into the URL fragment after the
        <code>#</code>. The site does not store those experiment states in a CMNA
        database. A URL fragment is normally handled by the browser and is not sent
        to the web server as part of an HTTP request. If you copy and share such a
        URL, however, anyone who receives it can see the experiment state encoded in
        the link.
      </p>

      <h2>Cookies</h2>
      <p>
        CMNA uses one necessary cookie to remember your privacy choice and, only with
        your consent, Google Analytics cookies.
      </p>

      <h3>Consent cookie</h3>
      <p>
        The website stores a cookie named <code>cookieConsent</code> after you accept
        or reject analytics cookies. The cookie records your decision and the version
        of the cookie notice under which it was made.
      </p>
      <p>
        The consent cookie is used only to remember the choice you requested. It is
        normally retained for 365 days and may be replaced sooner if you change your
        choice or if this notice changes materially.
      </p>

      <h2 id="cookie-settings">Cookie Settings</h2>
      <p>
        Google Analytics is optional. It will not load, contact Google, or set
        analytics cookies unless you affirmatively select <strong>Accept analytics</strong>.
        You may select <strong>Reject analytics</strong> without losing access to any
        part of CMNA, including the R laboratories.
      </p>
      <p>
        You may review or change your choice at any time by selecting
        <strong>Cookie Settings</strong> in the footer. If you withdraw consent, CMNA
        disables future Analytics collection and attempts to remove the Google
        Analytics cookies associated with this website from your browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your browser sends a recognized Global Privacy Control signal, CMNA treats
        that signal as a rejection of optional analytics.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="#cookie-settings" data-cookie-preferences>Open Cookie Settings</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Google Analytics</h2>
      <p>
        With your consent, CMNA uses Google Analytics 4 to understand how visitors
        find and use the site. This includes ordinary page measurement and selected
        interactions with the teaching laboratories, such as running a method,
        playing an iteration, exporting R, opening an experiment in the Workbench,
        viewing canonical source, or opening a prediction worksheet.
      </p>
      <p>
        The purpose is to understand which parts of the book companion are useful and
        which teaching paths people actually use. CMNA does not use Google Analytics
        for targeted advertising.
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Cookie</th>
            <th>Purpose</th>
            <th>Typical duration</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>_ga</code></td>
            <td>Distinguishes one browser from another for statistical measurement</td>
            <td>Up to two years</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>_ga_*</code></td>
            <td>Maintains session information for a particular Analytics property</td>
            <td>Up to two years</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        Analytics is configured with advertising storage, advertising user data,
        advertising personalization, Google Signals, and advertising-personalization
        signals disabled. User-level and event-level Analytics data are retained in
        Google Analytics for no longer than 14 months.
      </p>
      <p>
        Google may process Analytics information on servers outside your country.
        Additional information is available in
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's Privacy Policy</a> and
        its explanation of
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites">how Google uses information from sites and applications that use its services</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>External websites and services</h2>
      <p>
        CMNA links to websites and services operated by others, including GitHub,
        CRAN, publishers, retailers, webR documentation, and jameshoward.us. Following
        a link allows the destination service to receive information from your browser
        under its own terms and privacy practices.
      </p>

      <h2>Disclosure and sale of information</h2>
      <p>
        I do not sell personal information, use it for targeted advertising, or
        knowingly share it for cross-context behavioral advertising. Information may
        nevertheless be processed by service providers that support hosting, security,
        resource delivery, or measurement of the website.
      </p>

      <h2>Security and retention</h2>
      <p>
        Information is retained only for as long as reasonably necessary for the
        purpose for which it was processed, subject to the practices of the relevant
        infrastructure providers. No method of transmission or storage is completely
        secure.
      </p>

      <h2>Your privacy rights</h2>
      <p>
        Depending on where you live and which laws apply, you may have rights to
        request access, correction, deletion, restriction, objection, withdrawal of
        consent, data portability, or review by an appropriate privacy or
        data-protection authority. These rights may be subject to legal limitations
        or exceptions.
      </p>

      <h2>Children's privacy</h2>
      <p>
        CMNA is intended for a general educational and professional audience and is
        not directed to children. I do not knowingly use this website to solicit or
        collect personal information from children.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes to this notice</h2>
      <p>
        This notice may be updated when the website, its services, or applicable
        requirements change. If a change materially affects the basis on which
        analytics consent was obtained, CMNA will invalidate the previous consent
        version and request a new choice.
      </p>

      <p><em>Last updated: September 23, 2026.</em></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
